{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi Ms. ___. I’m Dr. ___. I hear you came in from dialysis because of blurry vision and your blood pressure was very low in the ER. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:21:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m tired. My left eye is still blurry. And they told me my pressure was scary low."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense to be worried. In the ER your blood pressure was 58 over 38, and they gave you a small IV fluid bolus. It came up after that. Any dizziness, fainting, or chest pain now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:23:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No chest pain. I don’t feel dizzy right now, just weak."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:25:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ll keep checking your pressures closely, especially around dialysis. Do you remember if you’ve been taking your midodrine at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Honestly… I haven’t been taking it like I should. I wasn’t sure it was helping."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:27:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for telling me. Midodrine helps keep your blood pressure up, especially after fluid is removed during dialysis. We’re going to restart it here and make sure you’re on a schedule you can follow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is the low pressure why I feel so wiped out?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:30:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be a big part of it. Your blood test called lactate was elevated when your pressure was low, which can happen when the body isn’t getting enough blood flow. After fluids, the lactate improved, which is reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:32:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my breathing? I get short of breath walking, but it’s not new."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked a chest X-ray today and it didn’t show pneumonia or fluid in the lungs. Since it’s not worse than usual, we’ll keep an eye on it, but nothing suggests a new lung infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Let’s talk about the vision. You said the left eye has been blurry since the venogram anesthesia during your last admission, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:35:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I woke up and it was blurry. It hasn’t gotten worse, but it hasn’t gone away either. And my right eye is already bad from shingles."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:37:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right. Ophthalmology examined you in the ER. They found your left eye vision improves when they use a +2.75 lens, and the inside of the eye looked normal on the dilated exam. Their impression is that this is a temporary focusing problem—loss of accommodation—after anesthesia, and it should improve over days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:38:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a stroke?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Based on their exam, a stroke is unlikely because the blurriness corrects with a lens and the eye exam was otherwise normal. We did think about it because you’ve had low blood pressures and you rely on the left eye, but the findings point away from a brain cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:41:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I live alone. I’m scared to go home like this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:42:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s exactly why we’re admitting you—to monitor your blood pressure and make sure your vision is stable and you’re safe. We’ll also talk about supports for when you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:44:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any eye pain, redness, or discharge?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:44:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No pain. Just blurry."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Any fever or chills since you left the hospital last time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:46:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re also drawing blood cultures because of your recent MRSA bloodstream infection. So far, no signs of a new infection, and your chest X-ray is clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:49:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I still getting the vancomycin with dialysis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2132-07-10 17:50:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’re continuing the plan for vancomycin given with hemodialysis to complete the full course recommended after the catheter-related MRSA bacteremia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2132-07-10 18:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I also haven’t had a bowel movement in days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2132-07-10 18:06:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for mentioning that. Constipation can happen with pain meds like oxycodone and with being less active. We’ll start a bowel regimen and keep you comfortable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Overnight your blood pressure stayed low-ish but stable, and your repeat lactate came down to normal. Any dizziness when you got up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:11:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No dizziness. Still tired. Vision is the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:12:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ll keep midodrine twice a day. For the vision, ophthalmology expects gradual improvement. In the meantime, using over-the-counter reading glasses may help with focusing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What strength glasses?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:15:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They found it corrected with about +2.75. Many drugstores have +2.5 or +3.0—either may help until you can get a full outpatient eye exam."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:16:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is there anything I should do to protect my good eye?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:18:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—because you’re essentially monocular, we recommend protective eyewear: polycarbonate lenses and sunglasses when outside, and being careful with activities that could injure the eye."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re also continuing your HIV medications and your prophylaxis—Bactrim and weekly azithromycin—just as you were taking at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:21:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I noticed you changed my valacyclovir."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2132-07-11 09:22:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "In the hospital we’re using acyclovir instead of valacyclovir, but it’s the same idea—antiviral coverage. At discharge you’ll go back to your home valacyclovir."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. How are the vision and energy today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Maybe a tiny bit better? Still blurry up close. My blood pressure feels okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:32:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s encouraging. Your pressures have been more steady since restarting midodrine. We’ll keep aiming for your dialysis dry weight—around 73.5 kilograms—so we don’t pull too much fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My throat has been sore since that procedure, and this morning it felt like food got stuck."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:35:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks—given your history and immune status, that sensation can sometimes be from yeast in the esophagus. We’re going to start fluconazole: a higher loading dose today, then 100 mg daily for a total of 14 days, and we’ll see if symptoms improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:36:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that the same medicine I took before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:37:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s the same antifungal. Let us know if swallowing becomes painful, if you can’t keep food down, or if you develop fevers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. Also my skin itching is still bad."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2132-07-12 08:40:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see the excoriations. Your eosinophil count has been elevated before, and the Strongyloides antibody test came back negative. We’ll continue hydroxyzine as needed for itching, and we’ll arrange follow-up with Dermatology and Allergy to look for other causes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2132-07-13 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi Ms. ___. Dialysis team mentioned there was some clotting in the dialysis catheter recently and they needed a heparin dwell. Have you noticed any new pain or swelling in the left groin where the catheter is?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2132-07-13 10:06:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s a little tender and puffy there. I’m worried it’s infected again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2132-07-13 10:07:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a reasonable concern given what you just went through. We examined it and don’t see drainage, but because of the swelling we ordered an ultrasound of the left groin to look for any fluid collection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your ultrasound is back: it shows no focal fluid collection along the catheter tract, which argues against an abscess."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:51:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So why is it swollen?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:52:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It may be local irritation or swelling around the line, and there’s also concern that the catheter and your known IVC narrowing can cause turbulent flow and increase the risk of clotting around the catheter. The clotting episode during dialysis is part of why we’re taking this seriously."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:54:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What can you do about that? I can’t lose this access."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:56:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Because your access options are limited and we’re worried about thrombosis risk, we recommend starting warfarin, a blood thinner, to reduce the chance of clotting around the catheter."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:57:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Isn’t that dangerous? I’ve heard people bleed on that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2132-07-13 14:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Bleeding is the main risk, yes. That’s why we monitor it with a blood test called INR and adjust the dose. We’ll coordinate INR checks with your dialysis sessions so it’s closely followed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2132-07-13 15:00:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it also help my atrial fibrillation?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2132-07-13 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can. You’ve been in normal rhythm here, but you have a history of paroxysmal AF. In your case, anticoagulation is being started mainly for the catheter clotting concern, and it may also cover other potential reasons your cardiology team has discussed in the past."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2132-07-13 15:03:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need to change what I eat?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2132-07-13 15:05:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "With warfarin, the key is consistency with foods that contain vitamin K—like leafy greens—rather than avoiding them completely. We’ll give you written instructions, and the dialysis unit will help track your INR."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How did dialysis go today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It went okay. No big drop in pressure like before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:02:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Your blood pressures have been stable on midodrine. Your blood cultures have stayed negative, which is reassuring given your recent MRSA infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:03:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My swallowing is a little better since starting the fluconazole."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good—that response supports our suspicion of candidal esophagitis. You’ll need to finish the full 14-day course even if you feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:06:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the vision—will it really go back to normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2132-07-14 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We expect continued gradual improvement. You’ve already noticed a bit of change. Use over-the-counter reading glasses for now, and it’s important you follow up with ophthalmology within a week for a full exam, especially since you rely on the left eye."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2132-07-14 15:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Let’s review the discharge plan. You’re going home today. Continue midodrine twice daily to prevent low blood pressure, especially around dialysis. Continue vancomycin with hemodialysis to complete the planned course. Continue your HIV medications and prophylaxis. Continue fluconazole daily to complete 14 days. And start warfarin 4 mg daily with INR checks arranged at dialysis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2132-07-14 15:46:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What should make me come back to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2132-07-14 15:48:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Come back right away for fainting, severe dizziness, chest pain, new shortness of breath, fever or chills, worsening pain/redness/drainage at the dialysis catheter site, vomiting blood or black stools, or any sudden worsening of vision. We’ll also give you instructions about warfarin bleeding signs and your follow-up appointments."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2132-07-10 17:16:00",
    "admission_end": "2132-07-14 16:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with ESRD on hemodialysis via a left femoral tunneled catheter, HIV/AIDS on antiretroviral therapy and opportunistic infection prophylaxis, and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation was admitted after presenting from dialysis with persistent left-eye blurry vision that began after anesthesia for a recent venogram and with marked post-dialysis hypotension (SBP 58/38) and elevated lactate that improved after a small IV fluid bolus and resumption of midodrine. Ophthalmology found the visual disturbance correctable with a +2.75 lens and a normal dilated fundus exam, consistent with transient loss of accommodation after anesthesia; she had mild improvement and was advised to use over-the-counter reading glasses, follow monocular safety precautions, and obtain outpatient ophthalmology follow-up within one week. Blood cultures remained negative and chest radiograph showed no acute findings while she continued vancomycin with hemodialysis for her recent MRSA catheter-related bacteremia. During the stay she developed sore throat and dysphagia concerning for candidal esophagitis and was started on a 14-day fluconazole course with improvement. Given catheter dysfunction with clotting requiring heparin dwell and concern for thrombosis risk in the setting of complex access and IVC stenosis, she was started on warfarin with plans for INR monitoring during dialysis; left groin swelling was evaluated by ultrasound without fluid collection and improved prior to discharge.",
    "problems": [
      "Relative/iatrogenic hypotension after hemodialysis (improved with IV fluids and midodrine)",
      "Left-eye blurry vision likely due to loss of accommodation after anesthesia; monocular precautions and ophthalmology follow-up",
      "Dysphagia/sore throat treated empirically as candidal esophagitis with fluconazole",
      "End-stage renal disease on hemodialysis with complex vascular access; left femoral tunneled catheter dysfunction/clotting risk",
      "Anticoagulation with warfarin initiated for concern of catheter-associated thrombosis risk; INR monitoring planned",
      "Recent MRSA catheter-related bacteremia (continuing vancomycin with hemodialysis); blood cultures negative this admission",
      "HIV/AIDS on antiretroviral therapy and opportunistic infection prophylaxis",
      "Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (in sinus rhythm during admission)",
      "Eosinophilia with pruritus/excoriations; Strongyloides antibody negative; dermatology/allergy follow-up planned"
    ]
  }
}
